description:
SILENT WRONG (found by s540d while probing #4981, 2026-11-13; the same on the base 24988e35, so pre-existing -- NOT caused by #4981's cache): in a tr/// SEARCH or REPLACEMENT list an ESCAPED dash `\-` is a literal `-` in perl, never a range operator; PCL's expand-tr-chars (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp) reads `a\-c` as the range a-c and drops a leading `\-`.

Reproducer (perl 5.40.3 vs PCL, base and s540d tree identical):

    my $t = "a-b-c"; my $n = ($t =~ tr/a\-c/Z/); print "$n $t\n";
    my $u = "abc-"; my $m = ($u =~ tr/\-a/XY/); print "$m $u\n";
    my $v = "a-z"; my $k = ($v =~ tr/a\-z//); print "$k\n";

perl:              PCL:
    4 ZZbZZ            3 Z-Z-Z
    2 YbcX             1 Ybc-
    3                  2

Where: expand-tr-chars unescapes `\-` before (or without marking it for) the range pass.  Fix: an escaped character -- `\-` in particular -- is a literal in the range scan (perl's toke.c scan_const marks it so).  Guard: Pl/t/tr-01.t rows with the three lines above (perl's answers).  perl-tests/tr.t has no row for it (it passes 242 rows).  Probe file: s540d scratch/s540d/g/trdash.pl.
